    "subset_reason": "Issue #284 / DR-010. This record does NOT supersede the full-matrix record 20260910-010233-e8e2e46 and does not re-derive any measurement that record already carries: the manifest edit behind it is purely additive (three extra 'meas ... find ... at=1.45e-3' taps and the two measurements they feed, vref_spread_early and vref_converge), and no existing measurement, bound, deck parameter or sample point changed, so every per-corner value and every corner count in 20260910-010233-e8e2e46 remains the current full-matrix result. What this record adds is the first evidence for the NEW vref_converge gate, and the subset is chosen to be the strongest 4 points for that purpose rather than a convenience sample: the sf and tt process corners at BOTH temperature extremes at the 3.63 V supply, which contains (a) the global worst-case vref_spread corner of the whole 45-point matrix, sf/-40 degC/3.63 V at 13.661 mV, (b) the marginal failing corner tt/-40 degC/3.63 V at 1.152 mV, and (c) the sf and tt 125 degC/3.63 V corners, where vref_spread is exactly 0 V and the new gate's 1e-5 V numerical tolerance is therefore actually exercised. The temperature axis is retained in full precisely so the spread_check below is still a live check on corner-awareness, and the subset deliberately MATCHES the one the post-layout sibling record takes, so the two are directly comparable. The reason this is a subset at all is host capacity, stated plainly: this run is on a shared Linux x86_64 host under heavy concurrent load, where a single corner of this deck takes 20-30 minutes wall against the 53 s the full-matrix record measured on the Darwin arm64 machine it was minted on, making a 45-point serial re-run impractical here -- a 10-point subset was started first and abandoned for the same reason after its first corner took 37 minutes wall. A full-matrix re-run on faster or uncontended hardware is tracked as its own issue.",
